      <p>This policy explains what data Mata Ara uses, why it is used, how it is shared, and the choices available to you. It applies to the iOS app, its Apple Watch companion and widget, and this website.</p>

      <h2>Usage tracking stays on your devices</h2>
      <p>Mata Ara reads the network traffic counters that iOS makes available on the device. To show usage and charts, the app stores totals for data sent and received, historical snapshots, and the settings you choose: visible interfaces, names and order, allowance, period and any expiry date, and notification preferences. This is not a per-app traffic breakdown.</p>
      <p>This information is stored locally on the device. The app code does not create a Mata Ara account or send these counters, settings, or snapshots to a Mata Ara server. Usage alerts are local notifications scheduled by the app.</p>

      <h2>Sharing with the widget and Apple Watch</h2>
      <p>The widget shares the app’s on-device storage with Mata Ara. If the Apple Watch app is installed and paired, Mata Ara sends interface categories, usage totals, configured quotas, and the last update time to it through Apple Watch Connectivity. A transfer may be queued while the Watch is unavailable. These features display counters across your Apple devices; they do not create a Mata Ara server backup.</p>

      <h2>History and retention</h2>
      <p>Mata Ara retains hourly snapshots for up to 48 hours and daily snapshots for up to 93 days. They support charts that can display up to 90 days. The app removes old snapshots during maintenance; if iOS does not run the app for a while, cleanup may wait until a later refresh.</p>
      <p>In the app options, you can hide or reorder interfaces, recalibrate a counter, and change or remove an interface configuration. Removing an interface deletes its settings and current total, but does not delete historical snapshots already recorded for it; those expire according to the periods above. The app does not currently offer a command to immediately erase all history. Mata Ara cannot remotely access data stored only on your device. Removing the app and managing its local data are controlled by iOS; backups or copies managed by Apple are subject to Apple’s services.</p>

      <h2>In-app purchases</h2>
      <p>Purchasing and restoring access to full features uses StoreKit and Apple’s App Store. Apple processes payment through the Apple Account used for the purchase. Mata Ara uses transaction information provided by StoreKit to verify and restore access in the app; the app code does not create a Mata Ara payment account. See <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" rel="external">Apple’s privacy policy</a> for more about Apple’s data practices.</p>

      <h2>Advertising, Google Mobile Ads, and ATT choices</h2>
      <p>The current code integrates the Google Mobile Ads SDK. The main-screen advertising banner appears only when iOS tracking permission is authorized and full access has not been purchased. Apple’s App Tracking Transparency (ATT) request concerns tracking activity across other companies’ apps, websites, or services, including for advertising or measurement. Declining permission does not disable Mata Ara’s local network counters.</p>
      <p>Depending on SDK configuration, device, region, and user choices, Google may process information such as IP address, device or advertising identifiers, ad interactions, and diagnostic or performance data. Google describes these categories and may update them; see <a href="https://developers.google.com/admob/ios/privacy/data-disclosure" rel="external">Google’s iOS AdMob data disclosure documentation</a> and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="external">Google’s privacy policy</a>. You can change ATT permission in iOS Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Tracking.</p>
      <p>The AdMob identifiers in the current source code are Google test identifiers. Before release, the advertising configuration and App Store privacy answers must be verified, and this policy updated if needed. The presence of the Google User Messaging Platform (UMP) dependency in the project does not mean that a UMP consent flow is currently integrated in the app.</p>

      <h2>This website and messages you send us</h2>
      <p>This website is generated as static pages. Its code has no form, audience analytics, or advertising cookies. The site host may process technical data needed to deliver and secure the pages, depending on the deployment configuration.</p>
      <p>If you email us, we receive your email address and message contents so we can respond and handle your request. The email service used to receive your message may also process this information under its own terms. Do not include passwords, purchase receipts, network logs, or other sensitive data that is not necessary. We keep messages while responding and following up as needed, then delete them when no longer needed, subject to applicable legal obligations.</p>

      <h2>Your choices and requests</h2>
      <p>You can decline or withdraw ATT permission in iOS Settings. You can also manage Mata Ara notifications in iOS Settings and change quota and interface settings in the app. Historical snapshots expire under the periods above; removing an interface does not erase them immediately. For questions about this policy or privacy requests, contact <a href="mailto:paraita@paraita.io">paraita@paraita.io</a>. We cannot remotely erase local data from your device.</p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>This policy may be revised if the app, its SDKs, or its data practices change. The date at the top of this page shows when it was last published. If a material change occurs, we will communicate it through an appropriate channel for the change.</p>
